  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 247 AM CDT Mon Aug 10 2026\n\nAn anomalously strong ridge remains over the Four Corners\nregion this morning, but the Bermuda High is becoming the dominant\nfeature in our region as it retrogrades from the Atlantic to the \nGulf Coast. As it treks westward this week, it will become a \nstronger limiting factor for rain chances. High PWAT values near 2\ninches will persist in the region through midweek, so increasing \nheat will push our daily heat index values more reliably over 105 \ndegrees across the area today and onward. Showers and \nthunderstorms will bring spotty relief from the heat. Drier air \nwill wrap from the Gulf and southwestern US as the ridge becomes \nmore prevalent, and PWAT values will fall closer to 1.5 inches by \non Friday. Model guidance is favoring a new surge of tropical\nmoisture by Saturday into Sunday though, and the stout ridge and\ndeep moisture could result in more widespread significant \ndangerous heat (heat index values near or greater than 110\ndegrees) by the weekend. Overnight low temps will only fall into\nthe mid or upper 70s later in the week, worsening heat stress for\nvulnerable populations and those without air conditioning. And \nrain chances will creep back in from the south and east as \nhumidity increases there.\n\nResidents are reminded that heat is particularly dangerous for \nthe elderly, the sick, and those without access to reliable air \nconditioning. Be sure loved ones and neighbors have access to a \ncool place to stay in periods of more intense heat and humidity. \n/NF/",
